          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em von Neumann regular ring
            or
            %em absolutely flat ring
            (
            %a{:href => "#LombardiQuitté2010"}> Lombardi &amp; Quitté (2010)
            ) is one where every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} principal left ideal
            or principal right ideal is generated by a single
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
            in 1936 as part of his algebraization of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            The principal left ideals in a von Neumann regular ring form a sublattice of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            of all left ideals which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complemented+lattice"} complemented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+lattice"} modular lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            based on lattices: if the elements of a von Neumann regular ring are thought of as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %em von Neumann regular ring
            (abbreviated as vN regular ring below) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
